<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    No cleartext http, anywhere, and only the certificate authorities the system ships.

    This is what Android already does for an app at this target level; it is written down so
    that it is a decision rather than a default, and so that raising or lowering the target
    cannot change it quietly.

    Nothing the app does itself needs plain http:
      - Signal: https and wss only, to Signal's own hosts, against Signal's own pinned roots.
      - GIF search: https.
      - The captcha page when registering: https.
      - Picture messages (MMS): a carrier's message center is very often a plain http address,
        but the app does not open that connection. It hands the message to the phone's own MMS
        service (SmsManager.sendMultimediaMessage / downloadMultimediaMessage), and the system
        makes the request on the carrier's network, outside this app and outside this file.
        The older in-app download path in android-smsmms (service_alt) is only taken if the
        "system_mms_sending" preference is false, and nothing in the app sets it.
      - Desktop Sync listens for a browser; it makes no outgoing connection.

    If a carrier ever needs the in-app MMS path back, cleartext would have to be allowed here
    for that carrier's message center by name, with the reason beside it. Not app-wide.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
